WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.580
Every television fan gets used to the pain
of seeing their favorite series canceled,

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:11.260
and we've all seen shows die because of ratings,
budget, or cast problems. Sometimes, though,

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.450
the plug gets pulled for reasons that were
totally avoidable—and sometimes even downright

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.570
dumb.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.320
HOLY SET DESTRUCTION BATMAN

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.869
The '60s Batman series starring Adam West
isn't anyone's idea of hard-hitting superhero

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:27.829
entertainment, but it's a campy classic—and
one that might've stayed on the air longer

00:00:27.829 --> 00:00:33.010
than two years if the network hadn't gotten
a destructive itch. Execs at ABC weren't willing

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:36.780
to continue funding Batman after its first
two seasons, so they decided to sell the rights

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.170
to NBC. Meanwhile, NBC wanted to make sure
Batman's existing sets and props were part

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:46.000
of the deal. Unfortunately, during a brief
production hiatus between networks, an ABC

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.960
exec had the sets destroyed. The deal died,
and the Caped Crusader hung up his cowl.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.640
ROBIN HOOD KILLED ROBIN HOOD

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:58.249
Jonas Armstrong, star of BBC One's Robin Hood,
decided to vacate the lead role after the

00:00:58.249 --> 00:01:02.550
show's third season. But instead of recasting
the part with a new actor, the producers opted

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:07.200
to simply kill off the character. Since they'd
already killed off Robin's love Maid Marian,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.500
that left the supporting cast to carry the
show. Y'know, the one named after a guy who

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.670
was dead. Although Robin Hood's writing staff
was willing to try a revamp, the network decided

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.090
there was no point in continuing.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.860
YOUNG JUSTICE'S LOUSY TOY SALES

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:25.530
When Warner Bros. hit paydirt with Young Justice—which
posted triple-digit ratings increases for

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.549
its time slot on Cartoon Network—it seemed
safe to assume they'd want more than two seasons.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:35.020
But the show was axed it after only 46 episodes,
prompting outcry and widespread speculation.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.030
Despite great ratings, the real reason it
was canceled was frustrating to its fans.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.110
Funding for the show came from a merchandising
tie-in deal for Young Justice action figures

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.250
from Mattel. But when low sales killed the
toy line, the show quickly followed suit.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.439
It's unfair when a show that's supposed to
be a 30-minute toy commercial is actually

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:52.399
pretty great...but the toys aren't.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.760
KINGDOM HOSPITAL VS. CSI

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.509
Few places are scarier than hospitals, so
when horror maestro Stephen King made a deal

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:02.710
with ABC to write and produce the haunted
medical drama Kingdom Hospital, it seemed

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:07.030
like a perfect fit. Sadly, the show's sole
season was kind of a mess—not on-screen,

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:11.020
where it offered a solid batch of episodes
that earned two Emmy nominations, but behind

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.280
the scenes, where struggles between King and
the network doomed it. Convinced ABC wasn't

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.360
promoting Kingdom Hospital enough, King paid
for a print ad campaign—only to see his

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.280
efforts foiled when execs moved it to a different
timeslot. King learned yet again that while

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.720
ghosts are scary, what's truly terrifying
is dealing with network executives.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.720
CAST INTO CANCELLATION HELL

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:34.310
After Buffy the Vampire Slayer ended in 2003,
fans flocked to the show's spinoff, Angel,

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.940
which was entering its fifth season. After
years on the renewal bubble, Angel enjoyed

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:42.190
a post-Buffy ratings bump. Series creator
Joss Whedon approached WB Network president

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:46.200
Jordan Levin about an early pickup for season
six. Although Whedon's move wasn't purely

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.590
a power play—he was looking for extra security
for Angel's team, who'd endured years of passing

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:54.630
up gigs while waiting for last-minute renewals—the
Levin saw it as an ultimatum. The president

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.140
pulled the plug, all for the sake of throwing his weight around. Although the network later

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.780
admitted it was a mistake, it was too late
to revive Angel.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.080
Thanks for watching, and don't forget to like and subscribe if you want more!

